A focused task or piece of content that interrupts the page, in a centered modal.
[Appica docs](https://appica.dev/ui/components/react/dialog)

<%= embed UI::DialogPreview, :default %>

## Usage

```ruby
Dialog do
  DialogTrigger { |trigger| Button(**trigger, variant: :outline) { "Invite people" } }
  DialogContent do
    DialogHeader do
      DialogTitle { "Invite your team" }
      DialogDescription { "Send an invitation by email." }
    end
    DialogBody { … }
    DialogFooter do
      DialogClose { |close| Button(**close, variant: :outline) { "Cancel" } }
      DialogClose { |close| Button(**close) { "Send invites" } }
    end
  end
end
```

`DialogContent` is a native `<dialog>` opened with `showModal()`: it sits in the top layer (no portal), traps focus,
makes the page behind inert, closes on Escape and returns focus to the trigger. On top of that the `ui--dialog`
controller plays the enter / exit animation, closes on a click outside (unless `dismissible: false`) and steps a
parent dialog back while a nested one is open.

With Rails, a dialog is often rendered by the server: `Dialog(default_open: true)` opens on connect - a Turbo Stream
can append one to the page, or a form can re-render inside one with its errors. Dispatch `ui--dialog:open` or
`ui--dialog:close` on the root to drive it from a script. For a high-stakes confirmation use an
[Alert Dialog](<%= page_path(:alert_dialog) %>); for a panel from an edge, a [Drawer](<%= page_path(:drawer) %>).

